url: "https://laurelpoetry.com"
abstract: >-
  Laurel is a corpus of public domain English poetry in which every line carries a
  scansion and most poems carry an identified metre. It runs from Homer in English
  verse translation to the edge of the United States public domain in 1930, and it
  continues to grow, so the figures for any given release are kept in the repository
  rather than here.


  Every line is encoded twice over. One pattern records the stress that the words
  themselves settle, taken from a pronouncing dictionary together with the poem's own
  syllabification; the other records the stress that the poem's metre asks for. The
  difference between the two is where a poet departs from the measure, which is where
  most of the interest in metre lies, and a corpus that stores only one of them
  cannot show it. Syllables that the dictionary does not settle are marked as open
  rather than guessed at. Stanzas carry a rhyme scheme lettered from rhyme keys, so
  that a sonnet's ABAB and a ballad's ABCB are machine-readable.


  The metre is not looked up. Every candidate is built as a template (the four feet
  at every length, with the licences that real verse takes and the substitutions
  that English verse actually makes, such as the inverted first foot), and every
  syllable of the poem is tested against each. A poem whose verse will not settle is
  left without a metre rather than given a guess.


  The accuracy has been measured and is reported in full: against hand-annotated
  lines gathered from three gold corpora, on a half of that data held out from every
  design decision, against works whose metre is documented in published scholarship,
  and against forms such as the sonnet whose metre needs no annotation because the
  form settles it. That last test exists because the others cannot detect a scanner
  that begins offering confident wrong answers to poems that were never in doubt. The
  current figures, and the failures, are in the documentation.


  Every poem has a canonical reference on the CTS model that names its edition,
  because a citation to a poem is not reproducible unless it says which text it
  means, and a permanent identifier that survives retitling and re-parsing. Many
  poems carry line-level timings against public domain recordings, so that a reading
  can be followed line by line.


  The corpus is the material underneath laurelpoetry.com, a free reading room where
  the same data is set out for reading and teaching: rhyme lettered on the page,
  stress marked against the poem's own metre, hard words glossed from a period
  dictionary, editors' commentary, a short course in prosody, printable worksheets,
  and exercises in scanning and naming forms. Nothing there costs anything, and
  nothing is behind a login.


  The texts are in the public domain. The encoding, the scansion and the annotation
  are CC BY-SA 4.0. The method is documented in METHOD.md, including the places where
  it is weak: free verse is still given a metre that it does not have, and editions
  were chosen for being in the public domain rather than for being the best witness.


